[07:32.07]During the arctic winter from October to March, the average temperature in the frozen north typically hovers around minus 20 degrees Celsius.
[07:41.89]But this year the Arctic is experiencing much higher temperatures.
[07:46.98]On February the 20th the temperature in Greenland climbed above freezing or zero degrees Celsius and it stayed there for over 24 hours.
[07:56.70]Then on February the 24th the temperature on Greenland's northern tip reached six degrees Celsius.
[08:04.08]Climate scientists describe the phenomenon as stunning.
[08:07.80]Weather conditions that drive this bizarre temperature surge have visited the Arctic before.
[08:13.44]They typically appear about once in a decade.
[08:16.60]However, the last such increase in temperature took place two years ago.
[08:21.54]This is troubling as climbing arctic temperatures combined with rapid sea ice loss are creating a new type of climate feedback loop which could accelerate Arctic warming.
[08:33.36]Indeed, sea ice cover in the Arctic is melting faster than expected.
[08:38.26]Without those masses of cooling sea ice, warm air brought to the Arctic can penetrate further inland than it ever did before.
[08:46.76]The air can stay warmer longer too.
[08:49.72]This drives additional melting.
[08:52.34]Overall earth is warming at a rapid pace.
[08:56.50]2014 through 2017 rank as the hottest years on record and the Arctic is warming twice as fast as any place else on earth.
[09:06.54]This raises unique challenges for Arctic wildlife and indigenous people who depend on Arctic ecosystems to survive.
[09:15.28]Previously climate forecasts predicted that Arctic summer ice would disappear entirely by around 2060, but based on what scientists are seeing now the Arctic may be facing summers without ice within 20 years.
[09:38.12]Q9. What did climate scientists describe as stunning?
[09:56.36]Q10. What does the passage say about that temperature surge in the Arctic?
[10:15.24]Q11. What may occur in 20 years according to scientists' recent observations?
[10:34.78]A good dose of willpower is often necessary to see any task through whether it's sticking to a spending plan or finishing a great novel.
[10:44.60]And if you want to increase that willpower.
[10:46.40]A new study suggests you just simply have to believe you have it.
[10:52.12]According to this study, what matters most is what we think about our willpower.
[10:57.74]If we believe it's a finite resource, we act that way, we feel exhausted and need breaks between demanding mental tasks.
[11:06.36]However, people who view their willpower as a limitless resource get energized instead.
[11:13.34]The researchers used a psychological assessment tool to test the validity of the study.
[11:20.40]They asked 1100 Americans and 1600 Europeans to grade different statements such as after a challenging mental activity, my energy is depleted and I must rest to get it refueled again or I can focus on a mental task for long periods without feeling tired.
[11:40.88]Although there was little difference between men and women overall.
[11:45.26]Americans were more likely to admit to needing breaks after completing mentally challenging tasks European participants on the other hand claimed they were able to keep going.
[11:57.18]Based on the findings, the researchers suggest that the key to boosting your willpower is to believe that you have an abundant supply of it.
[12:06.12]Your feelings about your willpower affect the way you behave.
[12:10.12]But these feelings are changeable, they said.
[12:13.64]Changing your beliefs about the nature of your self-control can have positive effects on character development.
[12:20.78]This leads to healthier behaviors and perceptions of other people.
[12:33.06]Q12. What is often necessary for carrying through a task?
[12:51.58]Q13. What is the finding of the new study?
[13:09.72]Q14. What do we learn about European participants as compared with their American counterparts?
[13:31.34]Q15. What do the research say concerning people's feelings about willpower?
[14:19.54]Here is my baby niece Sarah.
[14:22.18]Her mum is a doctor and her dad is a lawyer.
[14:25.96]By the time Sarah goes to college the jobs her parents do are going to look dramatically different.
[14:31.84]In 2013, researchers at Oxford University did a study on the future of work.
[14:40.06]They concluded that almost one in every two jobs has a high risk of being automated by machines.
[14:47.56]Machine learning is the technology that's responsible for most of this disruption.
[14:53.26]It's the most powerful branch of artificial intelligence.
[14:56.58]It allows machines to learn from data and copy some of the things that humans can do.
[15:03.78]My company, Kaggle, operates on the cutting edge of machine learning.
[15:08.88]We bring together hundreds of thousands of experts to solve important problems for industry and academia.
[15:16.24]This gives us an unique perspective on what machines can do, what they can't do and what jobs they might automate or threaten.
[15:24.48]Machine learning started making its way into industry in the early 90s.
[15:31.18]It started with relatively simple tasks.
[15:34.22]It started with things like assessing credit risk from loan applications, sorting the mail by reading handwritten zip codes.
[15:43.70]Over the past few years, we have made dramatic breakthroughs.
[15:48.38]Machine learning is now capable of far, far more complex tasks.
[15:52.46]In 2012, Kaggle challenged its community to build a program that could grade high school essays.
[16:01.38]The winning programs were able to match the grades given by human teachers.
[16:06.40]Now given the right data, machines are going to outperform humans at tasks like this.
[16:13.02]A teacher might read 10000 essays over a 40-year career.
[16:18.34]A machine can read millions of essays within minutes.
[16:21.28]We have no chance of competing against machines on frequent high-volume tasks, but there are things we can do that machines cannot.
[16:31.88]Where machines have made very little progress is in tackling novel situations.
[16:37.66]Machines can't handle things they haven't seen many times before.
[16:41.92]The fundamental limitation of machine learning is that it needs to learn from large volumes of past data.
[16:48.42]But humans don't.
[16:50.68]We have the ability to connect seemingly different threads to solve problems we've never seen before.
[17:07.80]Q16. What did the researchers at Oxford University conclude?
[17:25.98]Q17. What do we learn about Kaggle companies winning programs?
[17:45.56]Q18. What is the fundamental limitation on machine learning?
[18:05.98]We've talked recently about the importance of sustainable energy.
[18:10.64]We've also talked about the different theories on how that can be done.
[18:15.00]So far, our discussions have all been theoretical.
[18:19.44]Now I have a practical question for you all.
[18:22.00]Can you run a 140,000 kilogram train on just the steam generated by solar power?
[18:30.46]Well, one engineer, Tim Casselman, believes it's possible.
[18:35.84]And his home city of Sacramento, California should see the technology's first test as part of the upgrading of its rail yard.
[18:44.54]Casselman, who is an inventor and self-proclaimed steam visionary, is campaigning for a new steam train that runs without any fire and could run on an existing 10 kilometer line drawing tourists and perhaps offering city commuters a green alternative to their cars.
[19:03.80]Casselman wants to build an array of solar magnifying mirrors at one end of the line to collect and focus heat onto water filled tubes.
[19:12.10]This would generate steam that could be used to fill tanks on a small steam train without the use of fire.
[19:19.90]Supplying power to trains in this way would offer the shortest distance from well to wheels he says with the least amount of energy lost.
[19:30.18]According to Harry Valentijn, a Canadian engineer who is researching modern steam technology, a special tank measuring 2 by 10 metres could store over 750 kilowatt hours of energy as high pressure steam enough to pull a two car train for an hour or so.
[19:50.82]Energy to drive a steam locomotive can be stored in other materials besides water, for example, a team at Tohoku University in Japan has studied materials that can store large amounts of heat when heated.
[20:05.90]These materials turn from a solid into a liquid absorbing energy as they change phase.
[20:12.50]The liquid is maintained above its melting point until steam is required at which point the liquid is allowed to turn back into a solid releasing its stored energy.
[20:24.02]Another team at Nagoya University in Japan has tested calcium compound as an energy storage material.
[20:33.52]Heating this chemical compound drives off carbon dioxide gas, leaving calcium oxide.
[20:40.68]The gas can be stored under pressure and attain to recover the energy.
[20:45.76]The gas is fed back over the calcium oxide.
[20:49.34]In theory, says Valentijn, this can create a high enough temperature to generate superheated steam.
